  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nIdeas:\n- Use a bilevel optimization to simultaneously learn step function patterns and control their parameters.\n- Employ SAT-Solver constraints to enforce step function regularity and periodicity.\n- Leverage quantum annealing to escape local minima in the highly multimodal landscape.\n\nOriginal algorithm limitations:\n- Overly reliant on gradient free local searches\n- Fixated on specific initial configuration\n- Insufficient parameter sweeps across scale/scales of steps\n\nThis approach is substantive because:\n1) Combines two very different optimizers and adds constraint logic\n2) Parameters are separated into geometric (step heights, widths) vs temporal (auto-correlation)\n3) New feasibility constraints for step functions that were previously hand-specified\n\nImplementation-wise:\n- Represent h as sum of localized functions with parameters\n- Use SAT + quantum annealing to explore global structure\n- Apply gradient descent on selected parameters with repair functions\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\nVariables: (n_widths, n_heights, n_centers) where n_widths is scale parameter, n_heights is height, n_centers is x-position\n\nObjective:\nMinimize max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n\nParameterized form:\nh(x) = \u03a3_i (\u221a(2\u03c0)/\u03c3_i) * exp(- (x - \u03bc_i)\u00b2/(2\u03c3_i\u00b2)) * a_i  [normalized to \u222bh=1]\n\nConstraints:\n- 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 at all x\n- \u222bh dx = 1\n- Each component must have positive width and height\n\nEquivalence to original:\n- The integral can be evaluated via Gauss-Legendre quadrature\n- The sum of shifted integrals \u2261 multiple overlapping normal pdfs\n\nConstraint handling:\n- Projected gradient descent on the (\u03bc_i, \u03c3_i) space when bounds hit\n- Repair functions when h(x) < 0 or >1: use reflection and smoothing\n- Add small positive noise to standard deviations for Steinhart-Taylor scaling\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\nInitialize:\n- Load parent at 540 resolution if available\n- Create random set of candidate parameters:\n   * \u03c3_i: uniform(0.01, 0.5)\n   * \u03bc_i: uniform(-1, 3)\n   * a_i: uniform(0.01, 0.3)\n\nthen:\ndef compute_layer_correlations(h, dx):\n   n = h.size\n   corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n   return np.max(corr)*dx\n\nCore optimization loops:\n\nLoop1 (SAT-QA hybrid):\npruned_candidates = []\nfor each candidate set:\n   value = compute_layer_correlations(h)\n   # Add repair steps if h violates bounds\n   if value < best_value:\n      best_value = value\n      save candidate\n   QA_annealing(quantum_steps)  # 250 steps at start\n\nLoop2 (Gradient descent):\nlocal_opt = optimize_local(candidate)\nif better: replace candidate\n\nuntil time_budget or convergence\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n\nKey hyperparameters:\n- Layer count: 8-12 with schedule: \n     outer: initial random, then merge duplicates\n     inner: fix \u03bc_i and optimize \u03c3_i, a_i\n\nApple variation:\n- Random removal of some layers to explore sparser designs\n- Quantum annealing parameter sweeps (step size)\n- Gaussian process noise injection between QA steps\n\nTerminology rules:\n- Repair stricter than current code: first project to sum constraint, then independently enforce 0-1 bounds with stochastic rounding\n- Only compare objective directly on the candidate if no violations\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n\nClock time estimates:\n- Initial candidate generation: 15 seconds\n- Each QA iteration: 0.4 seconds (wall clock)\n- Gradient evaluations: 1.2 seconds each\n\nResource planning:\n- Divide budget automatically across resolution, layer count, parameter optimization levels\n\nRobustness measures:\n- Quantum annealing: prevent stuck states with stochastic restarts every 100 steps\n- Repair layers: after each repair, do 3 local gradient steps to stay ambitious\n\nFallback plan:\n- If all else fails, return parent solution with projection\n- If solution violates integral constraint, return trimmed parent\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\nFinal checks:\n1. assert 0.0 <= h[i] <= 1.0 for all i\n2. assert abs(np.sum(h)*dx - 1.0) < 1e-6\n3. Re-compute correlation with np.correlate with full mode\n\nImplementation contract:\n- All helper functions outside run() must lack IO\n- Feasibility check uses the project_to_sum function from parent code\n- Gradient calculations are explicit derivatives for each term\n\nSpecifically for h generation:\n\ndef generate_candidate(rng, n):\n   # Generate n_params = (layers, \u03c3_i, mu_i, a_i)\n   layers = np.random.randint(8,13)\n   sigma_i = rng.uniform(0,1, size=layers)\n   ...\n   return h\n\nTrust decisions on mathematical soundness of:\n- Use of multi-modal optimizer\n- Layer structure for representation\n- Objective function equivalence",
